A||||11||BroCar 87||Anyone ever changed the door locks on their fiero????||||1||
Z||000000||BroCar 87||05-07-2000||10:26 PM||||I bought my 87 coupe and didn't get a key for the doors. Is it hard to change the locks?? Is there a way to get the keys from the dealer? or something... Just lookin at my options.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroGTGirl||05-07-2000||10:45 PM||||I know my hubby rekeyed our locks a couple years ago, so it can't be that hard.<P>otherwise check out: <A HREF="http://www.fierostore.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fierostore.com/</A> <P>If you don't see locks at website ask for them to mail you a FREE catalog.  The locks are in the catalog for sure.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||BroCar 87||05-07-2000||11:05 PM||||WOW  only been on here a couple days and already giving advice... Thanks fierogtgirl||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroGTGirl||05-07-2000||11:18 PM||||I have had a Fiero for 5 years.  Have had to do research in the process.  My car is 14 years old, so parts fail.  <P>It's just nice to actually talk to others that have done the work and know from their experience...<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||PAUL88GT||05-07-2000||11:56 PM||||The lock cylinders can be removed by removing the inner door panels, then removing the retaining clips. I hope you have small hands. It is tight. Perform a search in the tech section for more info. Later||||reg||1
Z||000005||Loki||05-08-2000||12:40 AM||||Changing the lock cylinders is real easy... But you do need small hands (or just long fingers)  Once you get the inner panel off grab a flashlight and pick up at the lock, you will see exactly what you need to do. Easy and straight forward. I changed both cylinders in my 85 gt in about 30 minutes.. But I also have tiny hands (helps alot)   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Just e-mail me any questions ya have..<P>bigsexy@tyler.net||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||05-08-2000||10:10 AM||||A set of 2 lock cylinders is about 20 bucks at any Autozone, etc.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Phil||05-08-2000||11:02 AM||||Don't forget the trunk! If your battery ever goes dead you're sunk without a key||||reg||1
Z||000008||lawrence||05-08-2000||10:45 PM||||Since you are going through the trouble, you might consider making all your keys the same (including the ign key).  It's a little work, but it sure is nice having only one key for all the locks.<P>I wrote an article on what I did on my car.  Look in the Archives if you are interested.<P>Good luck,<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe</A>  - WebElectric Magazine||||reg||1
Z||000009||Monkeyman||05-09-2000||10:56 AM||||Ignition key has always been different on  any of the GMs I've ever owned.  Looks like FoMoCo actually did do something right.||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||05-09-2000||08:10 PM||||I'd rather have the doors and ignition be one key and the trunk a diferant one. then parking attendants can't get in there.||||reg||1
Z||000011||BN Boomer||05-09-2000||10:43 PM||||Hey FieroGTGirl, why don't you and your hubby come on up to Snohomish and help me finish getting the V6 out of my 85 GT? It 15 years old and parts fail. It's also getting frustrating!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
